[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Template Method vs. Strategy Pattern: Choosing the Right Behavioral Design Pattern"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When designing software systems that require variations of an algorithm while maintaining a consistent overall structure, developers often choose between the Template Method pattern and the Strategy pattern. Although both patterns deal with algorithm variation, they achieve this through fundamentally different mechanisms: inheritance versus composition."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Template Method Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Template Method pattern is a behavioral design pattern that defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a superclass, deferring some steps to subclasses. It lets subclasses redefine certain steps of an algorithm without changing the algorithm's structure."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mechanism: Relies on class inheritance and polymorphism."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control Flow: The parent class controls the execution flow (Hollywood Principle: "Don't call us, we'll call you")."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Coupling: Tight coupling between the abstract class and its concrete subclasses."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"abstract class DataExporter {
    public final void exportData() {
        readData();
        formatData();
        writeData();
    }
    abstract void readData();
    abstract void formatData();
    void writeData() {
        System.out.println("Writing data to default storage...");
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Strategy Pattern"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Strategy pattern is a behavioral design pattern that turns a set of behaviors into objects and makes them interchangeable inside original context objects. It lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mechanism: Relies on object composition and interfaces."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control Flow: The client chooses and injects the strategy into the context, retaining control."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Coupling: Loose coupling through well-defined interfaces."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"interface CompressionStrategy {
    void compress(String fileName);
}

class ZipCompression implements CompressionStrategy {
    public void compress(String fileName) {
        System.out.println("Compressing " + fileName + " using ZIP.");
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences: Inheritance vs. Composition"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most significant difference lies in how code reuse and variation are structured. Template Method uses inheritance, which means subclasses are bound to the parent class at compile-time. If behavior needs to change dynamically at runtime, inheritance falls short. Conversely, the Strategy pattern uses composition, allowing you to swap out algorithms dynamically at runtime."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use Which?"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Template Method when you have an invariant skeleton of an algorithm with minor steps that vary, and you want to avoid code duplication across multiple subclasses."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Strategy when you have multiple variations of an entire algorithm, need to switch algorithms at runtime, or want to avoid complex conditional statements (like massive switch-case blocks)."}]
